      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p115-question-continuity-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Determine whether the following functions are continuous or discontinuous at the break point.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-a-function",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+1&\\text{if }x<0\\\\-4x+1&\\text{if }x\\ge 0\\end{cases}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Continuous at $x=0$: $f_L(0)=f(0)=f_R(0)=1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=-4(0)+1\\\\f(0)&=1\\\\f_L(0)&=2(0)+1\\\\f_L(0)&=1\\\\f_R(0)&=-4(0)+1\\\\f_R(0)&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\nAll three values agree, so $f$ is continuous at $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-b-function",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f(x)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{x}{x+4}&\\text{if }x<-1\\\\-\\dfrac{1}{3}&\\text{if }x\\ge -1\\end{cases}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Continuous at $x=-1$: $f_L(-1)=f(-1)=f_R(-1)=-\\frac{1}{3}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-1)&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\\\f_L(-1)&=\\frac{-1}{-1+4}\\\\f_L(-1)&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\\\f_R(-1)&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nAll three values agree, so $f$ is continuous at the break point $x=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-c-function",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f(x)=\\begin{cases}x^2+1&\\text{if }x\\le 0\\\\-1&\\text{if }x>0\\end{cases}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Discontinuous at $x=0$: $f_L(0)=f(0)=1$, but $f_R(0)=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=0^2+1\\\\f(0)&=1\\\\f_L(0)&=0^2+1\\\\f_L(0)&=1\\\\f_R(0)&=-1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe values do not all agree, so $f$ is discontinuous at $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p115-question-continuity-part-d-function",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f(x)=\\begin{cases}13-x&\\text{if }x<3\\\\10&\\text{if }x=3\\\\x^2+1&\\text{if }x>3\\end{cases}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Continuous at $x=3$: $f_L(3)=f(3)=f_R(3)=10$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(3)&=10\\\\f_L(3)&=13-3\\\\f_L(3)&=10\\\\f_R(3)&=3^2+1\\\\f_R(3)&=10\\end{align*}$$\n\nAll three values agree, so $f$ is continuous at $x=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
